WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.449
C++ applications should not implement custom cryptographic hash algorithms.

2
00:00:03.449 --> 00:00:10.348
Correct cryptography depends on the algorithm design, the implementation, the library configuration, platform behavior, updates, side conditions, and

3
00:00:10.348 --> 00:00:10.732
review.

4
00:00:10.732 --> 00:00:19.547
A digest function may look simple from the outside, but reproducing a secure and maintainable implementation is not a good use of application

5
00:00:19.547 --> 00:00:20.313
engineering time.

6
00:00:20.313 --> 00:00:25.296
Use approved cryptographic libraries, platform APIs, or internal wrappers maintained by the organization.

7
00:00:25.296 --> 00:00:31.045
Algorithm selection should follow project policy, compliance needs, interoperability requirements, and the specific use case.

8
00:00:31.045 --> 00:00:40.244
A file integrity digest, a stored password verifier, and an authenticated message tag should not all be forced through the same helper just because

9
00:00:40.244 --> 00:00:41.393
they produce bytes.

10
00:00:41.393 --> 00:00:45.609
Good wrappers make the intended construction explicit and reduce accidental misuse.

11
00:00:45.609 --> 00:00:50.209
They also give maintainers one place to update defaults when policy changes.

12
00:00:50.209 --> 00:00:52.508
Dependency versions and build configuration matter.

13
00:00:52.508 --> 00:01:00.557
A correct call site can still inherit risk from an outdated library, disabled provider, unsupported platform mode, or inconsistent encoding choice.

14
00:01:00.557 --> 00:01:08.223
Centralized wrappers can help by defining algorithm names, output formats, error handling, migration behavior, and test vectors in one place.

15
00:01:08.223 --> 00:01:10.905
The wrapper should stay thin and reviewable.

16
00:01:10.905 --> 00:01:17.421
It should guide teams toward approved use, not hide risky decisions behind a vague function named hash.

17
00:01:17.421 --> 00:01:24.320
Reviewers should be able to see which construction is being used and why it matches the security goal.
